---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Overview from '../figures/01-overview.astro';
import Binding from '../figures/02-binding.astro';
import Unlock from '../figures/03-unlock.astro';
import Wire from '../figures/04-wire.astro';
import KeySet from '../figures/05-key-set.astro';
import Lifecycle from '../figures/06-lifecycle.astro';
import Rotation from '../figures/07-rotation.astro';
import Rollback from '../figures/08-rollback.astro';
import Deploy from '../figures/09-deploy.astro';
import Curves from '../figures/10-curves.astro';
import Trust from '../figures/11-trust.astro';
import Alerts from '../figures/12-alerts.astro';
import Failure from '../figures/13-failure.astro';
import mark from '../../public/favicon.svg?raw';
---
<Layout title="keystone" description="keystone is a Tang server for clevis network-bound disk encryption, running as a Cloudflare Worker.">
  <header class="hero">
    <div class="hero-mark" aria-hidden="true" set:html={mark} />
    <div>
      <p class="hero-eyebrow">Tang server · Cloudflare Worker</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title">keystone</h1>
      <p class="hero-lede">keystone is a Tang server for clevis network-bound disk encryption. It runs as a Cloudflare Worker, speaks the protocol of stock <code>tangd</code> and serves many named key sets from one deployment. A bound disk unlocks at boot by reaching the Worker over HTTPS. Deleting a set's secret revokes it. <code>keyset</code> mints, checks, deploys and revokes the sets.</p>
      <p class="hero-lede">A <code>tangd</code> on your LAN suits hosts that can reach one. keystone is for hosts that cannot.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Overview</h2>
    <div class="cat-explainer figure-pair">
      <Overview />
      <p>A bound disk's initramfs reaches the Worker over HTTPS through Cloudflare's edge, which can apply a WAF rate limit. Each key set lives in its own Worker secret.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Binding</h2>
    <div class="cat-explainer figure-pair">
      <Binding />
      <p>clevis fetches the set's advertisement, verifies its signature against the pinned <code>thp</code>, and derives the disk key from the exchange public key and a fresh ephemeral key.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Unlock</h2>
    <div class="cat-explainer figure-pair">
      <Unlock />
      <p>At boot clevis adds a fresh ephemeral point to the stored one and posts the sum to <code>/&lt;name&gt;/rec/&lt;kid&gt;</code>. The Worker multiplies it by the exchange private key; clevis subtracts its ephemeral share and is left with the disk key.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">On the wire</h2>
    <div class="cat-explainer figure-pair">
      <Wire />
      <p>The disk key and the unblinded point never leave the host.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">A key set</h2>
    <div class="cat-explainer figure-pair">
      <KeySet />
      <p>A set is one signing key and one exchange key on one curve, held in one Worker secret.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Lifecycle</h2>
    <div class="cat-explainer figure-pair">
      <Lifecycle />
      <p>Sets are write-once: a live name never changes keys, so rotation mints a new name.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Rotation</h2>
    <div class="cat-explainer figure-pair">
      <Rotation />
      <p>Bind a second slot to the new set, reboot to prove it, then unbind the old slot and revoke the old set.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Rollback</h2>
    <div class="cat-explainer figure-pair">
      <Rollback />
      <p>Each Worker version carries the secrets it was deployed with, so a rollback past a revoke serves the set again. <code>--apply</code> redeploys from current secrets and drops it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">keyset deploy</h2>
    <div class="cat-explainer figure-pair">
      <Deploy />
      <p>Nothing deploys while a set is refused, including when the wrangler target's secrets disagree with <code>--url</code>. The commands are in <a href="/setup/">Setup</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Curves</h2>
    <div class="cat-explainer figure-pair">
      <Curves />
      <p>Measured CPU for one <code>rec</code>. P-521, the default, needs Workers Paid; P-256 fits the Free plan.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Trust</h2>
    <div class="cat-explainer figure-pair">
      <Trust />
      <p>Whoever controls the Cloudflare account controls the keys: any Worker version deployed on it can read the secrets, and every version keeps its own copy. Secrets cannot be read back through the API, and the disks never hold keystone's keys.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Alerts</h2>
    <div class="cat-explainer figure-pair">
      <Alerts />
      <p>A successful <code>rec</code> from a new source, a /24 or /48 with its ASN and country, mails one alert per set per hour through <code>send_email</code>; sources met meanwhile wait for the hourly digest. A failed alert never changes the unlock.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Failure</h2>
    <div class="cat-explainer figure-pair">
      <Failure />
      <p>If keystone is unreachable or the Free plan's daily quota is spent, clevis fails and the host asks for its passphrase. Revoking a stolen disk's set stops it unlocking from then on. Losing keystone costs a passphrase per disk at boot, never data.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
